1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:03,350
5 .09 in the morning, mountain daylight time,

2
00:00:04,090 --> 00:00:06,450
Saturday, June 13th, 2026.

3
00:00:07,910 --> 00:00:09,270
No one is watching Biscuit Basin.

4
00:00:09,710 --> 00:00:12,190
The boardwalks here have been roped off for two years.

5
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:16,810
But a camera is. And in 30 seconds of footage,

6
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:20,390
the ground north of a pool called Black Diamond tears open.

7
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:23,530
Three new vents rip through the earth,

8
00:00:23,870 --> 00:00:26,390
throwing boiling water and steam into the sky.

9
00:00:27,590 --> 00:00:31,610
This is Yellowstone. And what just happened here is only part of the story.

10
00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:36,850
Two months earlier, a separate team of scientists on the other side of the world had

11
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:41,650
just published something that quietly rewrote 30 years of the textbook answer for what

12
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:43,810
powers this entire volcanic system.

13
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:46,310
You're watching Mind Wired.

14
00:00:47,770 --> 00:00:51,230
This is the real story of the ground that just cracked open at Yellowstone.

15
00:00:51,670 --> 00:00:53,910
The new scientific answer for what's underneath it.

16
00:00:54,090 --> 00:00:58,390
And the real disaster from decades ago that still shapes how we talk about the whole

17
00:00:58,390 --> 00:01:03,210
thing. Chapter one. The machine nobody was watching.

18
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:06,870
At 5 .09 in 50 seconds that morning,

19
00:01:07,230 --> 00:01:11,530
a research camera at Biscuit Basin recorded something the park's own geologists wouldn't

20
00:01:11,530 --> 00:01:13,070
see in person for another day.

21
00:01:14,410 --> 00:01:18,930
A dark plume of steam jetted from the ground a few dozen meters north of Black Diamond

22
00:01:18,930 --> 00:01:23,770
Pool. Seismic and infrared sensors nearby registered the shock at the same instant.

23
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:28,070
When Yellowstone's park geologists reached the site the next day,

24
00:01:28,430 --> 00:01:31,650
they found three separate places where the ground had torn open.

25
00:01:33,110 --> 00:01:35,170
A northern vent, 61 feet long,

26
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:36,670
filled with water near boiling.

27
00:01:37,310 --> 00:01:39,330
A cluster of five small events in the middle.

28
00:01:39,670 --> 00:01:42,070
A southern fracture, 49 feet across.

29
00:01:42,470 --> 00:01:45,590
All of it venting water close to 200 degrees Fahrenheit.

30
00:01:47,010 --> 00:01:49,450
Rock was thrown only a few feet from each vent.

31
00:01:49,970 --> 00:01:51,450
By Yellowstone's own standards,

32
00:01:51,730 --> 00:01:56,510
this was a small event. Smaller than the explosion at this exact same spot two years

33
00:01:56,510 --> 00:01:59,210
earlier. Nobody was hurt.

34
00:01:59,610 --> 00:02:04,330
Nobody could have been. This part of the park had already been closed since 2024,

35
00:02:04,790 --> 00:02:06,990
after the last time the ground did this.

36
00:02:08,730 --> 00:02:12,270
Michael Poland is the scientist in charge of the Yellowstone Volcano Observatory.

37
00:02:12,990 --> 00:02:15,030
His explanation is almost anticlimactic.

38
00:02:16,690 --> 00:02:18,590
These are not uncommon events in Yellowstone,

39
00:02:18,970 --> 00:02:22,670
Poland said. They probably happen a few times a year somewhere in the park,

40
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:24,770
often completely unobserved.

41
00:02:25,270 --> 00:02:28,330
Every now and then, we get one in a place we can actually see.

42
00:02:29,990 --> 00:02:32,870
Downstream, the Firehole River ran milky gray for hours,

43
00:02:33,170 --> 00:02:34,610
carrying sediment from the new vents.

44
00:02:35,430 --> 00:02:38,370
Poland again. The river stayed like that just for a few hours.

45
00:02:39,910 --> 00:02:42,470
Then, days later, something new happened.

46
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:47,550
A section of ground between the vents collapsed and a fresh pool of boiling water opened

47
00:02:47,550 --> 00:02:48,750
where solid earth had been.

48
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:54,850
By June 18th, that pool was spouting boiling water 20 to 30 feet into the

49
00:02:54,850 --> 00:02:57,830
air in bursts lasting several minutes at a time.

50
00:02:59,330 --> 00:03:03,110
A month later, the park installed a permanent camera aimed at the new vents.

51
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:06,210
Yellowstone isn't guessing about what happens here anymore.

52
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:10,190
It's simply watching in real time as the ground keeps changing.

53
00:03:11,670 --> 00:03:14,450
Chapter two. The answer everyone learned was wrong.

54
00:03:15,770 --> 00:03:20,210
For decades, the standard explanation for what powers Yellowstone has been the same one

55
00:03:20,210 --> 00:03:21,830
taught in almost every classroom.

56
00:03:23,390 --> 00:03:28,410
A plume of superheated rock rising in a single column from nearly 1 ,800 miles down,

57
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:31,470
almost at the boundary between Earth's core and its mantle.

58
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:34,110
A hose of heat running straight up.

59
00:03:35,530 --> 00:03:40,650
In April of 2026, a team led by geophysicist Liu Lijian at the Institute of Geology

60
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:44,650
and Geophysics in China published a study that challenges that entire picture.

61
00:03:45,630 --> 00:03:49,730
Their finding, Yellowstone may not be sitting on top of a rising plume at all.

62
00:03:50,250 --> 00:03:53,030
Instead, a slow horizontal current of hot rock,

63
00:03:53,530 --> 00:03:56,190
a kind of mantle wind, is sweeping in sideways,

64
00:03:56,650 --> 00:04:01,450
driven by the buried, sinking remains of a tectonic plate that vanished millions of years

65
00:04:01,450 --> 00:04:03,930
ago. Picture it this way.

66
00:04:04,190 --> 00:04:06,330
Instead of a hose pointed straight up from the core,

67
00:04:06,790 --> 00:04:11,030
Yellowstone sits in the path of a slow -moving underground current heat arriving from

68
00:04:11,030 --> 00:04:12,490
the side, not from below.

69
00:04:13,530 --> 00:04:17,990
That sideways current pushes against thicker crust to the east and lighter crust to the

70
00:04:17,990 --> 00:04:22,450
west, stretching a channel between them that lets magma rise and pool in a broad,

71
00:04:22,970 --> 00:04:25,050
shallow zone. Not one deep column,

72
00:04:25,390 --> 00:04:27,030
but something closer to a wide,

73
00:04:27,490 --> 00:04:32,910
unevenly filled sponge. It means the source -feeding Yellowstone's heat may

74
00:04:32,910 --> 00:04:36,510
be closer to the surface than scientists assumed for 30 years.

75
00:04:36,970 --> 00:04:39,050
It does not mean an eruption is any closer.

76
00:04:39,610 --> 00:04:40,830
Those are two different questions,

77
00:04:40,830 --> 00:04:43,710
and the study answers only the first one.

78
00:04:44,990 --> 00:04:47,710
Chapter three, how big this thing actually is.

79
00:04:48,870 --> 00:04:50,910
The ground under Yellowstone has done this before,

80
00:04:51,370 --> 00:04:55,670
three times, on a scale that makes June's small explosion look like nothing at all.

81
00:04:57,090 --> 00:05:01,710
2 .1 million years ago, an eruption now called the Huckleberry Ridge Event carved out

82
00:05:01,710 --> 00:05:03,510
a caldera in what's now Idaho.

83
00:05:04,990 --> 00:05:07,510
1 .3 million years ago, a second,

84
00:05:07,790 --> 00:05:10,390
smaller eruption, the Mesa Falls Event,

85
00:05:10,670 --> 00:05:14,950
did it again nearby. 640 ,000 years ago,

86
00:05:15,230 --> 00:05:17,270
the third eruption, Lava Creek,

87
00:05:17,710 --> 00:05:23,090
emptied more than 240 cubic miles of rock and ash and carved out the caldera that tourists

88
00:05:23,090 --> 00:05:25,410
drive across today without ever noticing it.

89
00:05:26,470 --> 00:05:29,830
That caldera measures 53 miles by 28 miles.

90
00:05:30,270 --> 00:05:33,010
It is the largest volcanic caldera in North America,

91
00:05:33,230 --> 00:05:37,370
and in places, the magma beneath it sits only a few miles under your feet.

92
00:05:38,250 --> 00:05:42,610
That heat is why Yellowstone has more than 10 ,000 hydrothermal features,

93
00:05:43,210 --> 00:05:45,610
hot springs, mud pots, fumaroles,

94
00:05:46,110 --> 00:05:48,710
geysers, more than 500 active geysers,

95
00:05:49,270 --> 00:05:52,510
roughly half of every geyser on the planet in one place.

96
00:05:53,810 --> 00:05:58,350
A research well -drilled at Norris Geyser Basin measured 459 degrees Fahrenheit,

97
00:05:58,810 --> 00:06:00,470
just over 1 ,000 feet down.

98
00:06:00,850 --> 00:06:03,390
That's the heat this entire system runs on.

99
00:06:04,310 --> 00:06:09,630
The most famous of all of them was named in 1870 by an expedition that camped nearby

100
00:06:09,630 --> 00:06:12,930
and noticed one geyser behaving with unusual regularity.

101
00:06:13,350 --> 00:06:14,810
They called it Old Faithful.

102
00:06:16,170 --> 00:06:18,410
It erupts roughly every 90 minutes,

103
00:06:18,670 --> 00:06:22,710
sending 3 ,000 to 8 ,000 gallons of water over 100 feet into the air.

104
00:06:23,190 --> 00:06:24,950
It isn't the biggest geyser in the park,

105
00:06:25,330 --> 00:06:26,370
or even the most regular.

106
00:06:26,790 --> 00:06:29,810
It's just the one that made a habit of performing where people were watching.

107
00:06:30,690 --> 00:06:34,210
Chapter 4. The Night the Ground Moved.

108
00:06:35,250 --> 00:06:39,110
August 17, 1959. 1137 at night.

109
00:06:39,570 --> 00:06:41,530
A few miles from Yellowstone's western edge,

110
00:06:41,870 --> 00:06:46,000
the ground let go. This was not Yellowstone's volcano erupting.

111
00:06:46,730 --> 00:06:50,450
It was a fault. A real, ordinary earthquake fault,

112
00:06:50,710 --> 00:06:54,190
part of the same forces slowly stretching the American west apart.

113
00:06:54,790 --> 00:06:58,070
The distinction matters, and scientists are careful to make it.

114
00:06:58,970 --> 00:07:02,330
A magnitude near 7 .2 to 7 .3.

115
00:07:02,930 --> 00:07:05,550
Some older records cite it as high as 7 .5.

116
00:07:06,270 --> 00:07:08,210
Tour through the Hebgen Lake and Red Canyon faults.

117
00:07:09,730 --> 00:07:11,730
On the south wall of the Madison River Canyon,

118
00:07:12,110 --> 00:07:15,510
the shaking sheared loose an enormous slab of mountainside,

119
00:07:15,890 --> 00:07:18,890
an estimated 50 million cubic metres of rock,

120
00:07:19,270 --> 00:07:22,010
mud and debris moving at highway speed.

121
00:07:23,250 --> 00:07:24,670
It crossed the canyon floor,

122
00:07:24,870 --> 00:07:28,230
climbed part way up the far wall and dammed the Madison River.

123
00:07:28,650 --> 00:07:30,550
The lake it created is still there.

124
00:07:30,950 --> 00:07:32,090
They named it Quake Lake.

125
00:07:33,550 --> 00:07:35,070
Twenty -eight people died that night.

126
00:07:35,590 --> 00:07:37,470
Nineteen of them are still under that slide.

127
00:07:37,950 --> 00:07:38,990
They were never recovered.

128
00:07:40,550 --> 00:07:42,590
Anita Painter -Thon was 12 years old,

129
00:07:42,990 --> 00:07:45,250
camping with her family that night for her own birthday.

130
00:07:45,810 --> 00:07:48,730
Her mother was killed. Her father was critically injured.

131
00:07:49,290 --> 00:07:53,170
Decades later, she wrote a book naming every one of the 28 victims,

132
00:07:53,510 --> 00:07:58,570
one by one. Highway 287, running along the lakeshore,

133
00:07:58,770 --> 00:08:00,310
dropped into the water in sections.

134
00:08:00,950 --> 00:08:03,830
Fault scarps up to 20 feet high cut straight across the road.

135
00:08:05,290 --> 00:08:08,970
And inside the park, the shaking reached Yellowstone's plumbing directly.

136
00:08:09,570 --> 00:08:10,690
In the days that followed,

137
00:08:11,190 --> 00:08:14,150
289 hot springs erupted as geysers.

138
00:08:14,730 --> 00:08:17,850
160 of them with no record of ever doing that before.

139
00:08:19,150 --> 00:08:23,150
It's real proof of how connected this entire region is beneath the surface.

140
00:08:23,150 --> 00:08:26,210
It is not proof that the volcano caused the earthquake.

141
00:08:26,610 --> 00:08:28,710
It didn't. The two systems touch.

142
00:08:29,210 --> 00:08:30,370
They aren't the same thing.

143
00:08:31,930 --> 00:08:34,730
Chapter 5. What scientists are actually watching for.

144
00:08:36,250 --> 00:08:41,470
So, after a new crack in the ground and a study that rewrote 30 years of theory,

145
00:08:42,090 --> 00:08:43,790
where does that leave things right now?

146
00:08:45,010 --> 00:08:48,190
The Yellowstone Volcano Observatory publishes a monthly update.

147
00:08:48,590 --> 00:08:52,230
As of this August, 68 earthquakes recorded in the previous month,

148
00:08:52,230 --> 00:08:55,310
the largest, a magnitude 3 .3.

149
00:08:56,810 --> 00:09:00,830
Ground uplift that had been rising since the middle of last year stopped in January and

150
00:09:00,830 --> 00:09:03,090
hasn't resumed. The current alert level,

151
00:09:03,510 --> 00:09:05,730
normal. The current aviation colour code,

152
00:09:05,930 --> 00:09:08,930
green. The observatory's own words,

153
00:09:09,270 --> 00:09:10,850
no signs of an impending eruption.

154
00:09:11,490 --> 00:09:13,910
The system beneath Yellowstone is mostly solid rock,

155
00:09:14,250 --> 00:09:17,410
not the giant tank of liquid magma the movies love to imagine.

156
00:09:18,890 --> 00:09:21,370
USGS does publish a probability figure,

157
00:09:21,370 --> 00:09:25,230
roughly 1 in 730 ,000, in any given year.

158
00:09:25,810 --> 00:09:30,090
But the agency is upfront that this number comes from averaging just two gaps between

159
00:09:30,090 --> 00:09:32,830
three ancient eruptions. In their own words,

160
00:09:33,210 --> 00:09:35,510
that's hardly enough data to make a real judgment.

161
00:09:36,890 --> 00:09:39,210
You'll sometimes hear that Yellowstone is overdue.

162
00:09:39,610 --> 00:09:40,930
That framing doesn't hold up.

163
00:09:41,210 --> 00:09:43,650
The gaps between the three real eruptions weren't even.

164
00:09:44,090 --> 00:09:47,450
Roughly 800 ,000 years, then 660 ,000.

165
00:09:47,910 --> 00:09:49,930
There was never a schedule to fall behind on.

166
00:09:50,810 --> 00:09:53,270
Chapter six, the real story,

167
00:09:53,510 --> 00:09:55,710
not the hypothetical one.

168
00:09:56,250 --> 00:09:59,270
Almost every major science channel has made the same video.

169
00:09:59,750 --> 00:10:01,830
What would happen if Yellowstone erupted tomorrow?

170
00:10:02,710 --> 00:10:06,170
It's a fair question. It's also not what actually happened this year.

171
00:10:07,550 --> 00:10:09,310
What actually happened is smaller,

172
00:10:09,610 --> 00:10:12,030
stranger, and in some ways more interesting.

173
00:10:12,470 --> 00:10:15,630
A real, small, non -threatening explosion,

174
00:10:15,630 --> 00:10:17,110
caught only by a machine,

175
00:10:17,390 --> 00:10:21,710
at the exact same time scientists on the other side of the planet were quietly rewriting

176
00:10:21,710 --> 00:10:24,090
the answer to how this whole system works underneath.

177
00:10:25,850 --> 00:10:26,970
Neither event caused the other.

178
00:10:27,550 --> 00:10:28,930
Nobody credible claims that.

179
00:10:29,410 --> 00:10:31,530
But both are true, both are recent,

180
00:10:31,890 --> 00:10:35,870
and together they're a better story than another hypothetical countdown to doomsday.

181
00:10:37,350 --> 00:10:39,010
What stays with me, honestly,

182
00:10:39,570 --> 00:10:43,010
isn't the size of the caldera or the scary probability numbers.

183
00:10:44,250 --> 00:10:49,470
It's that a camera alone in the dark at five in the morning recorded the

184
00:10:49,470 --> 00:10:51,590
ground doing something it's done quietly,

185
00:10:51,890 --> 00:10:56,110
unwatched, a few times a year for longer than anyone has been counting.

186
00:10:57,470 --> 00:11:01,930
And it's that 30 years of the accepted answer for what's underneath it all just changed,

187
00:11:02,370 --> 00:11:06,930
quietly, in a journal, months before most people ever heard about it.

188
00:11:08,170 --> 00:11:09,490
Somewhere in Wyoming tonight,

189
00:11:09,710 --> 00:11:12,810
that same camera is still watching the same stretch of ground.

190
00:11:12,810 --> 00:11:16,230
So far, it hasn't needed to show anyone anything else.

191
00:11:17,750 --> 00:11:19,790
If the ground moving at Yellowstone got to you,

192
00:11:20,030 --> 00:11:23,930
wait until you see what almost happened underneath a different kind of pressure entirely,

193
